{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 1329,
    "dDurationMs": 4090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Sea level seems like a pretty easy concept,\nright? You just measure the average level"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5419,
    "dDurationMs": 4361,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the oceans and that's that. But what about\nparts of the earth where there aren't oceans?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 9780,
    "dDurationMs": 5309,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, when we say that Mt. Everest\nis 8850m above sea level, how do we know what"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 15089,
    "dDurationMs": 4110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sea level would be beneath Mt. Everest, since\nthere's no sea for hundreds of kilometers?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 19199,
    "dDurationMs": 4311,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If the earth were flat then things would be\neasy - we'd just draw a straight line through"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 23510,
    "dDurationMs": 3550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the average height of the oceans and be done\nwith it. But the earth isn't flat."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 27060,
    "dDurationMs": 3670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If the earth were spherical, it would be easy,\ntoo, because we could just measure the average"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 30730,
    "dDurationMs": 3710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "distance from the center of the earth to the\nsurface of the ocean. But the earth isn't"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 34440,
    "dDurationMs": 4759,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spherical - it's spinning, so bits closer\nto the equator are \"thrown out\" by centrifugal"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 39199,
    "dDurationMs": 4201,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "effects, and the poles get squashed in a bit.\nIn fact, the earth is so non-spherical that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 43400,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it's 42km farther across at the equator than\nfrom pole to pole. That means if you thought"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 48860,
    "dDurationMs": 3710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "earth were a sphere and defined sea level\nby standing on the sea ice at the north pole,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 52570,
    "dDurationMs": 4900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then the surface of the ocean at the equator\nwould be 21km above sea level."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 57470,
    "dDurationMs": 4399,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This bulging is also why the Chimborazo volcano\nin Ecuador, and not Mount Everest, is the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 61869,
    "dDurationMs": 2881,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "peak that's actually farthest from the center\nof the earth."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 64750,
    "dDurationMs": 4830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So how do we know what sea level is? Well,\nwater is held on Earth by gravity, so we could"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 69580,
    "dDurationMs": 3530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "model the earth as a flattened \u0026 stretched\nspinning sphere and then calculate what height"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 73110,
    "dDurationMs": 4140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the oceans would settle to when pulled by\ngravity onto the surface of that ellipsoid."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 77250,
    "dDurationMs": 4330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Except the interior of the earth doesn't have\nthe same density everywhere, which means gravity"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 81580,
    "dDurationMs": 4130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is slightly stronger or weaker at different\npoints around the globe, and the oceans tend"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 85710,
    "dDurationMs": 4020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "\"puddle\" more nearer to the dense spots. These\naren't small changes, either - the level of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 89730,
    "dDurationMs": 4410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the sea can vary by up to 100m from a uniform\nellipsoid depending on the density of the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 94140,
    "dDurationMs": 4350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Earth beneath it. And on top of that, literally,\nthere are those pesky things called continents"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 98490,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "moving around on the Earth's surface. These\ndense lumps of rock bump out from the ellipsoid"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 102730,
    "dDurationMs": 4340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and their mass gravitationally attracts oceans,\nwhile valleys in the ocean floor have less"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 107070,
    "dDurationMs": 2680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mass and the oceans flow away, shallower."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 109750,
    "dDurationMs": 4180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And this is the real conundrum, because the\nvery presence of a mountain (\u0026 continent on"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 113930,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which it sits) changes the level of the sea:\nthe gravitational attraction of land pulls"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 118210,
    "dDurationMs": 4010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more water nearby, raising the sea around\nit. So, to determine the height of a mountain"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 122220,
    "dDurationMs": 3500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "above sea level, should we use the height\nthe sea would be if the mountain weren't there"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 125720,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at all? Or the height the sea would be if\nthe mountain weren't there but its gravity"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 129560,
    "dDurationMs": 669,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "were?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 130229,
    "dDurationMs": 4531,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The people who worry about such things, called\ngeodetic scientists or geodesists, decided"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 134760,
    "dDurationMs": 3640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that we should indeed define sea level using\nthe strength of gravity, so they went about"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 138400,
    "dDurationMs": 4419,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "creating an incredibly detailed model of the\nearth's gravitational field, called, creatively,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 142819,
    "dDurationMs": 4631,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Earth Gravitational Model. It's incorporated\ninto modern GPS receivers so they won't tell"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 147450,
    "dDurationMs": 3830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you you're 100m below sea level when you're\nin fact sitting on the beach in Sri Lanka"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 151280,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which has weak gravity, and has allowed geodesists\nthemselves to correctly predict the average"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 155780,
    "dDurationMs": 4700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "level of the ocean to within a meter everywhere\non Earth. Which is why we also use it to define"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 160480,
    "dDurationMs": 3970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "what sea level would be underneath mountains...\nif they weren't there... but their gravity"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 164450,
    "dDurationMs": 7000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "were."
    } ]
  } ]
}
